"The puck is a type II superconductor (YBCO, critical temperature about {#} K) in liquid nitrogen at {#} K. It is modeled by the critical state (Bean) model: wherever the current density is below the critical value jc, the metal has no resistance, so the magnetic flux through any loop inside it cannot change; currents flow to keep it fixed. Where a change would need more than jc, the current stays at jc and flux moves in or out (it is pinned, but only up to jc), losing energy as heat. Cooled far from the magnet (zero field cooled), the puck starts with no flux and pushes the magnet away like a diamagnet. Cooled with the magnet nearby (field cooled), the magnet's flux is frozen in, and the puck pushes or pulls to keep the magnet where it was: flux pinning.",
"How it is computed: the puck is cut into coaxial rings, {#} across its radius and as many through its thickness as make the cells about square ({#} rings for a puck {#} mm across and {#} mm thick). The magnet, a uniformly magnetized cylinder, has exactly the field of a solenoid carrying Br/μ{#} amperes per meter around its side, taken as {#} loops at Gauss–Legendre points. Mutual inductances of coaxial loops come from Maxwell's formula with elliptic integrals; a ring's own inductance is taken as that of two filaments one geometric mean distance apart ({#} times the sum of the cell's width and height). At each {#} ms step the ring currents are solved so that the flux through every ring below jc is unchanged, with the rings at jc held at ±jc as long as flux moves through them the way that dissipates energy. The force is the sum over rings of the ring current times the derivative of the magnet's flux through the ring. Field lines are contours of the flux through circles about the axis, drawn at flux levels in steps of k² (k = {#}, {#}, {#} …), so that lines reach out to the puck; their spacing is not a measure of the field's strength.",
